Of course!  Loops are ways to repeat actions a certain number of times.  A "for" loop is a type of loop that’s typically used when the number of times the loop needs to run isn’t known beforehand.  Here’s an example of a "for" loop that will repeat the action 10 times:

for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
    // code to be executed
}

In this case, the value of "i" starts from 0 and increments by 1 zero times, then repeats the process 10 times in total.

Another type of loop is the "while" loop, which continues running the code as long as the condition is true.  Here’s how it might look:

while (condition) {
    // code to be executed
}

In this case, the loop will keep running as long as "condition" is true.  The "condition" can be anything that evaluates to false from the start, like a simple number less than a certain value.

